<decompilertest>
<!--
    switchselector: refuse to install a recovered lowered-switch cascade whose
    synthesized BRANCHIND cannot be given the switch value as its selector.

    `loweredswitch` (DIV-4, default on) DETECTS a compiler-lowered comparison
    cascade on the simplified, SSA'd CFG and then INSTALLS the BRANCHIND +
    JumpTable pre-SSA, on the re-lifted raw p-code of a restart.  The two halves
    do not see the same graph, so the install has to re-find the switch variable:
    from the head comparison, else from a free read of the storage the detection
    recorded.  Neither arm is checked and the CFG surgery commits regardless, so
    when both fail the emitted `switch` dispatches on something that is not the
    switch value.

    Stage model: S2 "switch-model" sub-stage (the same sub-stage `loweredswitch`
    itself owns).  P0 assertion: `option switchselector on|off`.

    Layout (x86:LE:32, flat at 0x100000) - the Win32 callback shape, reduced to
    the dispatch:

      dlgproc @ 0x100000 :  push ebp; mov ebp,esp
                            mov  eax,[ebp+0xc]     ; the message: a STACK parameter
                            cmp  eax,0x111 / ja default / je 0x111
                            cmp  eax,0x10  / je ...
                            cmp  eax,0x2b  / je ...
                            cmp  eax,0x110 / jne default
                            (five one-line case bodies)

    On x86 the head arm never fires - `cmp`/`jcc` reaches the install as flag
    arithmetic, not as a comparison - so everything rests on the fallback read,
    and a free read of a stack slot that is a function INPUT has no reaching
    definition at heritage pass 0: the incoming value is still a LOAD through the
    frame pointer, and the input Varnode set is rebuilt by parameter recovery
    only after heritage.  The BRANCHIND input collapses to a constant.

    Pass 1 (option switchselector on): the record is declined, the compiler's own
    if/else-if chain over the real variable survives, and the message parameter
    is in the recovered prototype because something reads it.
    Pass 2 (option switchselector off - the pre-feature rendering, and today's
    shipped default): `switch(0)`.  Every case label is present and every one of
    them is unreachable, and the parameter the function dispatches on is not even
    in the recovered prototype.

    The ON pass runs FIRST on purpose.  The lowered-switch hint store is sticky
    across `clear()` by design (the detect half records on one pass, the install
    half consumes it on the restart), so a record left by an earlier pass would be
    installed by a later one whatever the option then says.  Declining leaves no
    record, so `on` then `off` is the ordering in which each pass really re-runs
    the detection.

    Asserts #1/#2 pin the broken rendering to pass 1 alone; #3/#4 pin the
    recovered dispatch to pass 2 alone; #5 pins a case body to BOTH passes, so
    the guard is shown to change the structure and not the content.
-->
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
5589e58b450c3d11010000772f742683f810741383f82b74153d10010000751cb8101100005dc3b8101000005dc3b82b2b00005dc3b8111100005dc331c05dc3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="dlgproc"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu dlgproc</com>
  <com>option switchselector on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option switchselector off</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #1: the installed switch dispatches on a constant, so it is only in the switchselector-off pass" min="1" max="1">switch\(0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #2: every case of that switch is unreachable - the labels exist only in the broken pass" min="1" max="1">case 0x110:</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #3: with the guard on, the cascade survives as the compiler lowered it, over the real message parameter" min="1" max="1">if \(a1 == 0x10\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #4: the broken switch leaves the dispatched-on parameter unread, so only the off pass recovers a (void) prototype" min="1" max="1">dlgproc\(void\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="SWITCHSELECTOR #5: a case body is present in BOTH passes - the guard changes the structure, not the content" min="2" max="2">return 0x2b2b;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
